findings.body:
Any authenticated user can pass `accountId` in the request body and read a different account. The previous code always used `caller.accountId` from `auth(req)`; this change substitutes `body.accountId` with no check that the caller is support staff or otherwise authorized for cross-account access.

Failure mode: attacker authenticates as themselves, sends `{"accountId":"<victim>"}`, and receives the victim account payload from `Response.json(account)`.

Require an explicit staff/admin authorization check before honoring a client-supplied id (or move this to a separate staff-only endpoint). Non-staff callers must always resolve to `caller.accountId` only — e.g.:

```typescript
const body = await req.json().catch(() => ({}));
let id = caller.accountId;
if (body.accountId != null && body.accountId !== caller.accountId) {
  if (!caller.isSupportStaff) {
    return new Response("Forbidden", { status: 403 });
  }
  id = body.accountId;
}
```
